EXPECTED: DEFECT

REASON: Code gap is real (nginx has no WINDOW_UPDATE rate limit) but differential attribution shows TLS/framing overhead — not application-layer DoS. PING flood at same frame rate produces ≥50% of target CPU → control_delta/target_delta ≥ 0.50 → not attributed.

TARGET_IMAGE: nginx:1.26.0
TARGET_PORT: 443/tcp
CLIENT_TOOL: h2

PROOF_METHOD: differential_attribution
PROOF_DETAIL: |
  Target: WINDOW_UPDATE flood, ~317K frames/s, CPU threshold crossed (CONFIRMED in
  pre-differential-attribution pipeline run 20261112-173801-5eac).
  Control: PING flood at same rate — PING has O(1) server cost (one PONG per frame,
  no stream state). Control CPU delta ≈ 90% of target CPU delta.
  Ratio: control_delta / target_delta ≥ 0.50 → NOT attributed to application overhead.
  The O(1) WINDOW_UPDATE handler is indistinguishable from the O(1) PING handler at
  this throughput; the CPU is consumed by TLS recv/frame-parse overhead, not by nginx
  application logic. Pipeline verdict: DEFECT.

PROOF_NOTES: nginx's WINDOW_UPDATE handler at ngx_http_v2_state_window_update
             (src/http/v2/ngx_http_v2.c) does only one integer add + overflow check.
             The code gap is real (no rate limit) but is a conformance gap / defense-in-depth
             improvement, not an exploitable application-layer DoS. The bottleneck is
             the protocol framing layer, not nginx application logic.
             Verdict added: commit c26db0e (differential attribution).
